  E'Why numpy is fast',
  E'numpy stores arrays as contiguous C buffers and runs reductions as one C loop. The sum below operates over a million elements — same answer Python''s `sum()` would give, in a fraction of the time.',
  E'Run a sum of one million elements and recognise the printed value.',
  E'**Concept.** numpy stores arrays as contiguous C buffers — every adjacent element is one cache line away. A `.sum()` runs as one C loop with SIMD-friendly access; the L1 cache loads 64-byte runs and the CPU never stalls on memory. A Python list stores pointers to boxed `PyObject` ints scattered across the heap — every element costs a cache miss and an interpreter dispatch. Same arithmetic, completely different access pattern.\n\n**Example.**\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nx = np.arange(1_000_000)\n# Sum 0 + 1 + 2 + ... + 999999.\n# In numpy: one C loop, no per-element Python overhead.\n# In Python: 1,000,000 trips through the interpreter.\nprint(int(x.sum()))\n```\n\n**Predict the output.** Predict the printed sum of 0..999999. (Hint: n(n−1)/2 with n = 1,000,000.)\n\n**Expected.** `499999500000`',
  null,
