  'quant-16-boolean-filtering-on-real-prices',
  E'Boolean filtering on real prices',
  E'Trader on the desk asks: ''how many days did SPY gap up — open above yesterday''s close?'' Easy enough, the junior thinks. They ship a function. The trader runs it, gets a number that looks reasonable, and now the strategy sizes positions against that count. Except the function counts the wrong thing — a subtle index shift that makes the answer almost-but-not-quite right. The kind of bug a 30-second look-at-the-tape would have caught.',
  E'Find the off-by-one in the gap-up counter and fix it.',
  E'**Concept.** A gap-up day is when today''s *open* is above *yesterday''s* close — `df[''open''] > df[''close''].shift(1)`. The `.shift(1)` is the entire point: without it you''re comparing today''s open to today''s close, which is closer to ''is this a green candle?'' than ''is there a gap''. A real production bug pattern: someone wrote the comparison without the shift, the function compiles, returns a number, and the strategy quietly sizes against the wrong signal.\n\n**Find the bug.** The function looks like it counts gap-up days but doesn''t. Compare your output to the test expectation on the 4-row example, then read the docstring carefully — one method call is missing.\n\n**Expected.** All tests pass.',
