  '00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000318',
  '00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000033',
  'quant-25-bond-yield-to-maturity',
  E'Bond yield to maturity',
  E'The treasury desk at a primary dealer quotes prices on a hundred-strong universe of bonds; the buy-side queries the system in YTM space. The function below sits between those two views — given face, coupon, term, and current price, return the YTM. No closed form exists; you root-find. Same code shape ships at every fund running a fixed-income book.',
  E'Implement `ytm(face, coupon, n_years, price)` using scipy''s brentq root-finder.',
  E'**Concept.** An annual-coupon bond''s fair price is `sum(c / (1+r)**t for t in 1..N) + face / (1+r)**N`. The YTM is the rate `r` that makes this equation balance against the market price. `scipy.optimize.brentq(f, lo, hi)` does a robust bisection — needs `f(lo)` and `f(hi)` to straddle zero. A 0.0001..0.50 bracket covers any realistic bond. At par (price == face), YTM exactly equals the coupon rate — your cheapest sanity check.\n\n**Implement the function.** Implement `ytm`. Define the NPV closure that returns PV minus price; root-find with brentq on (0.0001, 0.5). The docstring spells out both steps.\n\n**Expected.** All tests pass.\n\n**Why this?** Bond markets quote in price; risk and portfolio analytics live in yield space. This function is the translation layer every fixed-income system runs millions of times a day.',
  null,
  null,
  '{}',
