  '00000000-0000-0000-0000-00000000031d',
  '00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000033',
  'quant-30-binomial-tree-pricer',
  E'Binomial tree pricer',
  E'Before Black-Scholes'' PDE became the standard solver in vol-desk software, the CRR (Cox-Ross-Rubinstein) tree was *the* pricing algorithm. It still is for American options — early exercise needs a backward induction that the closed-form BS can''t do. Every options engineer can sketch the recurrence on a whiteboard; today you write it as code.',
  E'Implement `crr_call(S, K, r, sigma, T, N)` — an N-step CRR binomial tree pricer for a European call.',
  E'**Concept.** Set `dt = T / N`, `u = exp(σ·√dt)`, `d = 1/u` (multiplicatively symmetric). Risk-neutral up-probability `p = (exp(r·dt) − d) / (u − d)`. Terminal prices at expiry: `S · u^i · d^(N−i)` for `i = 0..N`. Terminal call payoffs: `max(S_T − K, 0)`. Backward-induct: at each step, `vals_new = exp(−r·dt) · (p·vals[1:] + (1−p)·vals[:-1])`. After N iterations, `vals[0]` is the price at t=0. As N → ∞ the price converges monotonically to BS.\n\n**Implement the function.** Implement `crr_call`. The docstring spells out the six steps; the cleanest code is ~10 lines.\n\n**Expected.** All tests pass.\n\n**Why this?** American options need backward induction — Black-Scholes can''t price early exercise. The same tree machinery prices warrants, convertibles, and most early-exercise structured products.',
  null,
  null,
  '{}',
  '{"max_hint_level": 2, "do_not_reveal_solution": false, "encourage_tests_first": false}',
  array['quant', 'options', 'black-scholes'],
  true,
  3000
)
