  '00000000-0000-0000-0000-00000000032a',
  '00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000035',
  'quant-43-conditionals-and-loops',
  E'Conditionals and loops',
  E'The C for-loop is the syntactic ancestor of every C-family language''s for-loop — JavaScript, Java, Go, C#, Rust all stole this shape. Three clauses, separated by semicolons: init, condition, increment. The compiler knows enough about that structure to vectorise the body if it can. That''s why the hot loops in numpy, pandas, BLAS are all written like this.',
  E'Print the first 5 squares using a for-loop.',
  E'**Concept.** `for (init; condition; update) { body }` — three clauses, executed init once, condition each pass, update after each pass. `for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++)` runs the body with i = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. `i++` is shorthand for `i = i + 1` — every C-family language inherited it. `printf("%d ", x)` writes an integer followed by a space; no automatic newline.\n\n**Example.**\n\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n        printf("%d ", i * i);\n    }\n    printf("\\n");\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n**Fill in the gap.** Replace `___` with the increment that advances i by 1 each iteration.\n\n**Expected.** `1 4 9 16 25`\n\n**Why this?** Tight `for` loops the compiler can unroll and vectorise are how numpy, BLAS, and every hot path in a low-latency system actually run their arithmetic.',
  null,
  null,
  '{}',
  '{"max_hint_level": 2, "do_not_reveal_solution": false, "encourage_tests_first": false}',
  array['quant', 'c-language'],
  true,
  4300
)
